the real secret of everything comes from  this yellow pet hey guys welcome to my  nighttime routine I'm gonna be sharing  with you everything that I do before I  go to sleep as well as the scientific  reasoning for why I do it  I'm trying to get the best night's sleep  possible I want to drive down my anxiety  levels especially during a stressful  time and finally three I want to get  myself ready to face tomorrow as the  best version of myself before we get  started let's give a huge shout out to  Sensodyne for sponsoring this video  we're starting in the bedroom the  bedroom bedroom bedroom the reason why  I'm starting here even though it's not  bedtime just yet is because I want to  prep my sleep environment to be ready by  the time I get to bed I really focus on  three things here temperature humidity  and light levels these all need to be  set ahead of time preferably two three  hours so by the time you get to bed  you're not rolling around trying to fall  asleep that's gonna peak your anxiety  and anxiety is the enemy of good sleep  the first thing I do is I set the  temperature I ideally look for 65  degrees Fahrenheit the reason I do this  is cuz your body naturally lowers its  core temperature while you sleep so if  you can assist in the process by  lowering the environment temperature  you're gonna be doing yourself a favor  if you don't and it's warm in your room  your body's gonna try and cool down by  sweating and have you ever slept in  sweaty sheets that's a bad night's sleep  next I flip on my humidifier the air can  get quite dry in my apartment because I  am running the a/c and dry air really  irritates the nasal passageways leading  to congestion scratchy throat chapped  lips it's really difficult to get a good  night's sleep that way and I actually  recommend a humidifier to many of my  patients in order to keep their mucus  membranes voiced to potentially a  decrease the likelihood of them getting  a virus  the last piece to the puzzle is to get  rid of all external sources of light and  to make that happen I have to close my  blackout shades scientific research has  actually shown that even small sources  of light can make it difficult for you  to fall asleep  and to stay asleep I've made it a  complete blackout situation in my room  and I've really noticed the difference  over the last year or two we've wrapped  up in the bedroom let's take it to the  bathroom first things first  getting rid of the facial hair now I  used to shave before but then I realized  it made me look way too young to be a  doctor I kept getting those comments for  my patients there Doogie Howser who is  too much so now I use a beard trimmer  and really get that hair nice  short as well as using a shaver in order  to get rid of the scruff around my neck  I think it still looks professional but  at the same time it's a pretty aesthetic  look next I want to give you a sneak  peek at my skincare routine generally I  like to keep it pretty simple with a  Murad cleanser followed by a moisturizer  but on other days I'd like to get a  little fancy and I'm going to show you  that whole process when I do a dedicated  skincare video people look out for that  last but certainly not least is taking  care of these pearly whites look your  oral health is intimately connected to  the health of the rest of your body  we've actually seen with scientific  research that those who have poor oral  health have poor cardiovascular outcomes  as well you got to brush your teeth at  least twice a day something you may not  know about me is that I have really  sensitive teeth I've had braces twice in  my life whiten my teeth probably once  too much and because of that it's quite  painful to drink cold liquids like those  cookies and cream milkshakes something  I've been doing to combat it is using  sensitized sensitivity and gum  toothpaste because it truly helps this  is targeted for people who have  sensitive teeth or gum problems it's  actually the number one dentist  recommended brand for sensitive teeth  definitely check it out can't forget to  feed the new now then you just gotta  poop walking the new during quarantine  this is actually my favorite part of my  nighttime routine because it really  helps me wine down it's dark it's quiet  nobody's out the goes down except you  the bear finally peeps  now it's time for me to go upstairs and  share with you a couple of secrets that  I do to lower my anxiety before bed this  is when my wine down truly begins and  I'm gonna take you through the few steps  that I have laid out here of things you  need to do in order to get into bed and  fall asleep really quickly first things  first I know the lights in here aren't  that dim because I have the lights of  recording with but generally I only have  that light and that light on to keep it  dim so that when I get to bed my  melatonin levels are at their highest  point melatonin is a sleepiness hormone  that gets released when it's dark it's  part of your natural circadian rhythm so  the last thing you want to do is turn on  all the lights right before you go to  sleep and hurt that melatonin release  you want to keep down as high as  possible second I have my sleepy time  tea here I love green tea but green tea  has caffeine you don't want to consume  caffeine before bed  even if you're able to fall asleep it's  not a good idea because it'll interrupt  the stages of your sleep and you're  gonna wake up feeling not refreshed so  my sleepy time tea is chamomile no  caffeine it's an herbal tea there's  something relaxing about it I love it  it's my go-to drink next the real secret  of everything comes from this yellow pad  it's nothing to do with this yellow pad  it's just where I'm gonna write down the  next things I'm about to tell you first  create a to-do list for the following  day because the last thing you want to  do is get into bed and start thinking  all I have to remember to do this this  and this that's gonna keep you up get it  down on paper get it out of your mind  second I'm gonna tell you about the  three good things the three good things  is something I've wanted to tell you for  the longest time I wanted to make a  video dedicated to it because I'm so  excited about the three good things it's  actually a process meant to decrease  anxiety and depressive symptoms for up  to six months and all it takes is every  night before you go to sleep so grab a  yellow bowl or a non yellow pen or your  phone and to write down three good  things that have happened to you that  day as well as a brief reason as to why  for example for me number one I'll say  bear listen to me and the reason will be  lots of training it could be something  as small as that it could be something  as big as your family member had a  graduation you met someone someone held  a door for you in the supermarket or  yummy meal it doesn't matter as long as  it's positive the reason we're doing  this is we're retraining our minds to  let go of the anxiety and think about  the good things our minds naturally have  a baseline level of anxiety that's how  our ancestors survived but now it's much  safer you know we're living in homes  where there are no natural predators so  we need to think about the positive  things less so about the anxious things  the patients that I've recommended this  to say it's a true game changer I stand  behind it they stand behind it and it's  been researched in psychiatric medical  journals saying that it truly does work  come on it's bedtime for me but I'm  gonna leave you with three things that I  recommend you do not do before going to  bed first thing cell phone get the cell  phones away from you if you need to scan  for urgent emails from work or messages  that's fine but don't go on social media  check out what your ex is doing get FOMO  that's gonna peak your anxiety levels  definitely don't do that  second avoid heavy conversation you know  you get into bed with your partner and  